---
import ToolPage from '../../components/ToolPage.astro';
import AfterTaxCalc from '../../components/calc/AfterTaxCalc';
import StateTables from '../../components/StateTables.astro';
import { FUNDS, fund, pct, day, META, YIELD_DAYS } from '../../lib/data';
import { fiftyTest, stateRate } from '../../lib/engine/tax';
import { src, related, METHOD, PAGE_FUNDS } from '../../lib/common';
import { title as fitTitle, desc as fitDesc } from '../../lib/snippet';
const govs = FUNDS.filter((f) => ['government', 'treasury', 'treasury-only'].includes(f.k));
const fails = govs.filter((f) => fiftyTest(f.q) === 'fails');
const caMunis = FUNDS.filter((f) => f.k === 'state-muni' && f.st === 'CA');
const vct = fund('VCTXX');
const r93 = stateRate({ state: 'CA', filing: 'single', income: 120000 });
const title = fitTitle(['California Money Market Fund Tax: The 50% Rule by Fund', 'California Money Market Fund Tax: The 50% Rule, Fund by Fund']);
const description = fitDesc([
  `California money market fund tax: which funds passed the 50% quarter-end test on their filings, and what each yield leaves after California and federal tax.`,
  `California money market fund tax: which funds passed the 50% quarter-end test in their filings, and what each fund’s yield leaves you after California and federal tax.`,
]);
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Are money market fund dividends taxable in California?', a: `Yes, unless they come from U.S. or California obligations held by a fund that kept at least 50% of its assets in such obligations at the close of each quarter of its tax year. Then the part of the dividend from those obligations is exempt, and you subtract it on Schedule CA (540).` },
  { q: 'Where do I find the exempt percentage for California?', a: `In the fund company’s year-end tax information, often a table titled U.S. government obligations or state tax information. Many companies also say there whether the fund met California’s 50% requirement for the year. Use that figure, not the holdings estimate on this site, on your return.` },
  { q: 'Is a California municipal money fund better than a Treasury fund?', a: `It depends on your brackets. On the filed yields VCTXX paid ${pct(vct.y)} free of both federal and California tax, while VUSXX paid ${pct(fund('VUSXX').y)} free of California tax only. The calculator above compares the two for your income; at most incomes on these yields the Treasury fund kept more.` },
  { q: 'Does California tax repo income of a money fund?', a: `Yes. Interest on repurchase agreements is paid by the bank or dealer that borrowed, not by the United States, so it is not interest on U.S. obligations even when Treasuries are the collateral. It also does not count toward the 50% of assets the fund needs at each quarter end.` },
];
---
<ToolPage id="california" title={title} description={description} crumb="California"
  h1="California money market fund tax: the 50% rule, fund by fund"
  lede={`Yields as filed for ${YIELD_DAYS}, holdings as of ${day(META.month)}, California rates for tax year 2025. Not tax or investment advice.`}
  faqs={faqs} related={related('fifty-percent-rule', 'fund-vctxx', 'vmfxx-vs-vusxx')} sources={src('ftb-ca', 'ftb-ca-rates', 'nmfp', 'irs-rp')}>
  <AfterTaxCalc client:load funds={PAGE_FUNDS} a="VUSXX" b="VCTXX" methodHref={METHOD} defState="CA" id="c-ca" />
  <div class="prose prose-slate mt-10 max-w-none">
    <h2>What California exempts, and when</h2>
    <p>California taxes money market fund dividends as ordinary income, with one exception that the Franchise Tax Board spells out in the Schedule CA instructions. If a fund held at least 50% of its total assets in obligations of the United States or of California at the close of each quarter of its taxable year, the part of its dividends that came from those obligations is exempt. Interest on Treasuries counts; so does interest on debt of the Federal Home Loan Banks and Farm Credit Banks, which federal law shields from state tax. Repurchase agreements do not count, nor does debt of Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. The test is all or nothing at the fund level: a fund at 49% on one quarter end gives its shareholders no exemption for the year, even if most of its income came from Treasuries. In the filings behind this site, {fails.length} of the {govs.length} retail government and Treasury share classes were below 50% at the June 30 or September 30 quarter end. For a Californian in the {pct(r93, 1)} bracket at $120,000 of income, that difference alone is worth about {pct(r93 * fund('VMFXX').y * fund('VMFXX').usob)} of yield on a fund like VMFXX.</p>
    <p>Single-state municipal funds are the other route: their dividends come from California issuers and are free of both federal and California tax for residents. The data holds {caMunis.length} California municipal share classes. Their yields are lower, and the calculator above starts with VCTXX against the Treasury fund VUSXX so the trade-off is visible at your income.</p>
  </div>
  <StateTables st="CA" />
</ToolPage>
